# 3. Global approximate synchronization is support-decidable

A stochastic matrix is **scrambling** iff every pair of rows has overlapping support.

Equivalently,

\[
\delta(P)<1.
\]

The Dobrushin coefficient is submultiplicative:

\[
\delta(PQ)
\le
\delta(P)\delta(Q).
\]

### Theorem CW-002E.1 — Global zero synchronization iff a scrambling product exists

\[
\boxed{
v_{\rm glob}(\Sigma)=0
\iff
\exists w:\delta(P_w)<1.
}
\]

### Proof

If \(v_{\rm glob}=0\), choose a word with

\[
\delta(P_w)<1.
\]

Conversely, if

\[
c:=\delta(P_w)<1,
\]

then repeated use of the same word gives

\[
\delta(P_w^k)
\le
c^k
\to0.
\]

QED.

### Decidability

For nonnegative matrices,

\[
\operatorname{supp}(PQ)
=
\operatorname{supp}(P)
\odot
\operatorname{supp}(Q)
\]

under Boolean matrix multiplication.

Whether a product is scrambling depends only on this support matrix.

There are at most

\[
2^{n^2}
\]

Boolean support matrices.

Therefore one can compute the finite Boolean support semigroup generated by \(\operatorname{supp}(P_a)\) and check whether it contains a scrambling pattern.

Hence:

\[
\boxed{
v_{\rm glob}(\Sigma)=0
\text{ is decidable.}
}
\]

A shortest support witness has length bounded by the number of distinct reachable Boolean support states, hence by \(2^{n^2}\).

Chevalier–Gusev–Jungers–Hendrickx develop stronger algorithms/bounds for related SIA/scrambling/convergent-product questions. The elementary support argument above is sufficient for the zero-value dichotomy.

