# 4. Probabilistic automata

A probabilistic finite automaton consists of:

- finite state set \(Q\);
- alphabet \(A\);
- rational row-stochastic transition matrices \(M_a\);
- rational initial distribution \(\alpha\);
- accepting set \(F\subseteq Q\).

For

\[
w=a_1\cdots a_k,
\]

acceptance probability is

\[
\operatorname{Acc}_{\mathcal A}(w)
=
\alpha M_w\mathbf 1_F.
\]

Its value is

\[
\operatorname{val}(\mathcal A)
=
\sup_w
\operatorname{Acc}_{\mathcal A}(w).
\]

The **value-1 problem** asks whether

\[
\operatorname{val}(\mathcal A)=1.
\]

Gimbert–Oualhadj proved this undecidable.

The cut-point emptiness problem asks, for rational \(\lambda\),

\[
\exists w:
\operatorname{Acc}_{\mathcal A}(w)>\lambda.
\]

This is also classically undecidable in general.

