# 9. No Černý-style recursive witness bound in the weighted problem

Define the \(\varepsilon\)-pair reset threshold

\[
\boxed{
L_\varepsilon(\Sigma;p,q)
=
\inf
\{
|w|:
TV(pP_w,qP_w)<\varepsilon
\},
}
\]

with \(L_\varepsilon=\infty\) if no such word exists.

Suppose there were a computable function

\[
B(I)
\]

of the encoded instance \(I=(\Sigma,p,q,\varepsilon)\) such that whenever

\[
L_\varepsilon(I)<\infty,
\]

one had

\[
L_\varepsilon(I)\le B(I).
\]

Then one could decide finite-threshold pair synchronization by enumerating all words of length at most \(B(I)\).

This contradicts Theorem CW-002E.4.

Therefore:

### Corollary CW-002E.5 — No recursive universal witness-length bound

\[
\boxed{
\text{There is no computable a priori finite witness bound for general weighted pair synchronization.}
}
\]

This is a sharp contrast with deterministic synchronizing automata, where the reset-threshold problem has finite combinatorial bounds and Černý asks for the optimal quadratic one.

The probabilistic weighted extension does not merely make the bound larger.

It destroys recursive boundedness altogether.

