# 10. A striking support-versus-weight phase transition

Combine Theorems CW-002E.1 and CW-002E.3:

\[
\boxed{
\text{global all-row approximate synchronization}
\quad\text{is decidable},
}
\]

while

\[
\boxed{
\text{designated-pair approximate synchronization}
\quad\text{is undecidable}.
}
\]

Why?

## Global zero synchronization

The condition reduces to existence of a single scrambling product:

\[
\delta(P_w)<1.
\]

That is a support property.

Once one strict contraction exists, repetition drives the global row diameter to zero exponentially.

## Pair zero synchronization

Two selected distributions can approach each other through arbitrarily delicate probability-weight effects without any finite support event certifying convergence.

The problem retains the full numerical expressiveness of probabilistic automata.

Thus:

\[
\boxed{
\text{support geometry is finite-state;}
\qquad
\text{weight geometry can encode undecidable limits.}
}
\]

This is one of the cleanest byproducts of the Cold War Renaissance interaction so far.

